<speak>
<s>From <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="zʊhd">zuhd</phoneme> news, this is your briefing for <say-as interpret-as="date" format="dmy">30032026</say-as>.</s>
<break time="1s"/>
<prosody rate="95%">
<s>Iran struck Kuwait's largest desalination plant overnight, killing one worker and marking the first direct Iranian attack on Gulf civilian infrastructure since this war began.</s> <s>Kuwait desalinates nearly all its drinking water — this wasn't collateral damage, it was a missile aimed at a nation's survival infrastructure.</s> <s>The Gulf states have spent months trying to stay neutral, but Trump's pause on Iranian energy strikes expires April sixth, and the clock is now everyone's problem.</s>
</prosody>
<p>
<s>In politics.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>Iran's wartime leader Mojtaba Khamenei has still not appeared in public since assuming power, communicating only through written statements — his latest thanking Iraq's religious leadership for their support.</s> <s>In the Shia political tradition, the Supreme Leader's visible presence is itself a source of legitimacy, and vanishing during wartime erodes precisely the authority the office demands.</s> <s>Trump told the Financial Times he believes the leader is dead or gravely injured, and Tehran has offered nothing to contradict him.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Trump lands in Beijing tomorrow for the first US-China summit since twenty seventeen, arriving at a table where Xi Jinping holds leverage he didn't have before.</s> <s>China's been stockpiling Iranian crude throughout the war, positioning Beijing as the one power with economic sway over both Washington and Tehran simultaneously.</s> <s>Trade tariffs are formally on the agenda, but whether Xi uses his oil leverage to broker a ceasefire or to extract concessions from a distracted America will define the visit.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Ahmed al-Sharaa — the former rebel commander the EU once sanctioned — arrived in Berlin today as Syria's president, seeking reconstruction funds in exchange for something Chancellor Merz badly wants: refugee returns.</s> <s>A million Syrians in Germany lost their legal asylum grounds the moment Sharaa ousted Assad, and Berlin sees their return as both a political prize and a logistical challenge.</s> <s>Whether this becomes an orderly resettlement or a political expulsion depends on what Sharaa secures at the table.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Netanyahu ordered an expansion of Israeli military control in southern Lebanon while the world watches Iran.</s> <s>Israeli strikes have killed over twelve hundred people, displaced a million, and destroyed eight bridges over the Litani River — what Lebanon's president calls preparation for permanent occupation.</s> <s>The timing isn't coincidental... every escalation in the Gulf buys another week of silence on what's happening north of the Israeli border.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Sheikh Abduljabbar Kabara says his blasphemy death sentence in Kano was political retaliation — that court dates were altered and legal filings tampered with across a three-year appeal he's been systematically denied.</s> <s>His petition to Nigeria's Chief Justice now tests whether federal constitutional protections can override a sharia court's capital verdict.</s> <s>It's a question Nigeria's dual legal system has avoided answering for decades, and a man's life hangs on the answer.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Islamabad's Capital Development Authority is bulldozing a twenty-five-year-old Christian settlement — clearing homes without resettlement plans, in open defiance of a twenty fifteen Supreme Court order protecting informal settlement residents.</s> <s>Pakistan's Human Rights Commission has demanded a halt, but the demolitions continue uninterrupted.</s> <s>A court order designed to shield the most vulnerable is being ignored by the very state apparatus that issued it.</s>
</p>
<p>
<s>On the economy.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>Brent crude hit a hundred and fifteen dollars a barrel — its highest since twenty twenty-two — driven by the Kuwait strike and intensifying Houthi attacks across the Red Sea.</s> <s>Japan's Nikkei fell four and a half percent as Asian markets opened to a Monday morning reality no one wanted: this war is escalating, not winding down.</s> <s>Oil is now on track for its biggest monthly gain on record, and every importing nation is recalculating what the next quarter costs.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>India's central bank capped every bank's net dollar position at a hundred million dollars — a wartime capital control, not a routine monetary adjustment.</s> <s>The rupee had fallen four percent since the war began, and with crude making up eighty percent of India's imports, the pressure on the currency isn't speculative — it's structural.</s> <s>Banks have until April tenth to comply, but the intervention signals that the Reserve Bank sees the rupee as dangerously exposed to forces beyond its control.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>South Africa's Auditor-General found sixty-eight billion rand in military assets that cannot be located or verified — qualifying the defence force's accounts for the ninth consecutive year.</s> <s>This week, twenty-two hundred soldiers deploy in an eight-hundred-and-twenty-three-million-rand crime operation, and parliament hasn't received a budget breakdown.</s> <s>A military that can't account for its own weapons is being sent to police the streets — with equipment no auditor can confirm exists.</s>
</p>
<p>
<s>In science.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>A multi-method study has established a causal link between bisphenol A — the plastic chemical in receipts, food containers, and water bottles — and major depressive disorder.</s> <s>Researchers mapped five hundred and seventy-one shared gene targets and confirmed four causal proteins in synaptic and neurodevelopmental pathways, moving decisively beyond correlation into mechanism.</s> <s>BPA exposure is nearly universal in industrialized countries, yet neither American nor European regulators have updated their safety thresholds in light of the findings.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Researchers boosted carbon dioxide capture by twenty-five percent using the simplest amino acid — glycine — impregnated into activated carbon at post-combustion temperatures.</s> <s>The method can be retrofitted into existing fossil fuel plants without redesign, which matters because the world's dirtiest power stations aren't closing tomorrow.</s> <s>Glycine costs roughly two dollars per kilogram — the cheapest building block of life, repurposed to clean up its industrial consequences.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>RNA sequencing of brain metastases from breast cancer patients has revealed that tumors reaching the brain carry fewer killer T-cells and far more immunosuppressive macrophages than the original tumor.</s> <s>This explains a painful clinical mystery: why checkpoint inhibitors — the most promising class of cancer immunotherapy — consistently fail once cancer crosses into the brain.</s> <s>Thousands of patients face this treatment gap every year, and now researchers finally understand the biological reason behind it.</s>
</p>
<p>
<s>In technology.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>Philadelphia's courts have banned all smart eyewear starting next week — Meta Ray-Bans, Google equivalents, everything — citing AI-powered recording and facial recognition risks before any specific misuse occurred in its buildings.</s> <s>No other American court has pre-emptively restricted an entire product category like this.</s> <s>It's the first legal boundary drawn around wearable surveillance in a courtroom, and it likely won't be the last.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>AI coding agents may be reviving the free software movement by making it economically viable for individuals to maintain and fork projects that currently require entire corporate teams.</s> <s>Free software won in infrastructure — Linux, PostgreSQL — but lost in applications because volunteer maintenance couldn't compete with paid engineering at scale.</s> <s>If agents can sustain a fork cheaply, the power dynamic between corporate maintainers and the communities they built on shifts in ways we haven't seen since the nineties.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>A European petition is demanding that EU governments block Palantir from public procurement, arguing the American surveillance firm already holds defence and intelligence contracts giving it access to the continent's most sensitive systems.</s> <s>No EU law currently bars foreign surveillance contractors from winning government work.</s> <s>The petition demands one — and whether Brussels acts will reveal how seriously Europe takes digital sovereignty when the contractor is an ally.</s>
</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<prosody rate="90%">
<s>That's your briefing from <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="zʊhd">zuhd</phoneme> news.</s>
</prosody>
</speak>